This is the NEWS file for RCC, the Regoshi C Compiler. It summarizes
user-visible changes for each release, most recent first. For the
complete list of changes see the git log.

* Changes in v1.2.4 (????-??-??) (Reini Urban)

  Optional libdwarf support: if the Makefile finds libdwarfp (header +
  library; probe result in HAVE_LIBDWARF, override with `make
  HAVE_LIBDWARF=0|1`), -g builds .debug_line/.debug_info/.debug_abbrev/
  .debug_aranges through it
  (src/obj_libdwarf.c); otherwise, or if libdwarf fails, the built-in
  emitter in src/obj.c is used.

  Debug levels now follow gcc: -g and -g1 (the default -g level) emit the
  line table only; -g2 (-ggdb2) and -g3 (-ggdb3) emit full DWARF 4 (ELF
  x86-64 with libdwarfp, src/dwarf_g3.c; elsewhere both silently behave like
  -g1), -g3 additionally with macros: subprograms
  with parameters, locals in lexical blocks (compound statements and for
  initializers), globals, typedefs, base/pointer/array/function types,
  cv-qualifiers, struct/union/enum with bit-fields, and #define/#undef as
  .debug_macinfo (gdb `info macro`/`macro expand`). Every section-relative
  reference carries a real relocation, so several -g3 objects link
  correctly; rcc's own linker keeps the debug sections. Not yet covered:
  block-scope statics, VLAs, TLS variables, register-promoted locals (no
  location), typedef names on variable types.

  -g (every level, ELF x86-64) now also emits .eh_frame call-frame
  information for rcc's functions (src/eh_frame.c): backtrace(), C++
  exceptions, pthread_exit() and gdb's bt/finish work through rcc frames, and
  locals resolve from the first instruction (DW_AT_frame_base is the CFA).
  rcc's own linker writes .eh_frame_hdr and PT_GNU_EH_FRAME.

* Changes in v1.2.3 (2026-11-06) (Reini Urban)

  -O2 SSA IR backend (x86-64 ELF, x86-64 Windows/COFF, AArch64 ELF and Darwin):
  - New Win64 x86-64 emitter path (mingw/PE): rcx/rdx/r8/r9 or xmm0-3
    by argument position, 32-byte shadow area, rsi/rdi callee-saved,
    xmm4/xmm5 scratch, aggregates <= 8 bytes by value in the position's
    register (larger ones by pointer / hidden return pointer), variadic
    float duplication and home-area spill, ___chkstk_ms probing of
    frames >= 4 KiB, SEH unwind records for IR functions. coff_write now
    emits the in-place addend of rip-relative references with a symbol
    offset or a trailing immediate; the TLS (emutls) call relocation is no
    longer recorded during the dry-run pass.
  - Every eligible function (scalar integer/pointer/float/double code —
    loops, branches, calls, switch, goto, statement expressions,
    struct/union/array access incl. aggregates by value) is lowered to
    an SSA IR, optimized, and compiled with linear-scan register
    allocation; unsupported constructs (inline asm, atomics, volatile
    aggregate copies, complex/decimal/__int128/long double, C2y labeled loops,
    VLAs, cleanups, nested functions, sanitizer/-g/-fstack-protector
    TUs) fall back per-function to the regular code generator. New
    flags -fno-ir, -fno-ir-inline, -fdump-ir.
  - New AArch64 SSA emitter with scalar and floating-point instruction
    selection, linear-scan allocation with spills and callee-saved
    `x19`-`x28`/`v8`-`v15` preservation, VALUE8/HFA/pointer aggregate
    ABI support, `x8` sret, Darwin stack varargs, ELF and Mach-O
    ADRP/GOT relocations, paired saves, scaled/extended addressing,
    `madd`/`msub`, and `cbz`/`tbz` branch fusion. Unsupported calls
    still fall back before emission on a per-function basis.
  - Optimizations: same-file inlining of small callees (honours
    __attribute__((noinline)), which the AST inliner now also honors),
    tail recursion to loops, loop rotation, induction-variable widening
    (wrap-freedom proven by a sound range analysis; no UB assumptions),
    scaled strength reduction (stride 12/24/40 struct arrays), GVN
    with redundant-load elimination, store-to-load forwarding and
    dead-store elimination with escape analysis of non-escaping frame
    slots, invariant-load hoisting, noreturn shrink-wrapping (cold
    assert/abort paths no longer force a frame or callee-saved saves on
    the hot path), branch-free abs/labs/llabs, and memory operands in
    ALU/FP ops (add/sub/and/or/xor/imul/cmp/SSE arith/ucomis).
  - volatile accesses (scalars, pointers, members, arrays, bitfield
    units, compound assignment and ++/--) are modelled in the IR as
    observable loads/stores that no pass removes, merges, hoists,
    narrows or reorders; the IR compare for <, <=, >, >= now signals
    invalid on a NaN operand (comiss/comisd) like gcc.
  - Linux benchmarks: six-workload suite at -O2 executes in 454 ms
    (was 693; TCC 548, GCC -O2 196); the 14-benchmark AWFY suite in
    5000 ms (was 6060; TCC 5837, GCC -O2 3310), all outputs verified.

  Better linkers:
  - Security: non-`-shared` links no longer get an unconditionally
    executable stack (PT_GNU_STACK RWE); only requested when a
    linked-in object actually needs one (nested-fn trampoline) (GH #63).
  - Implemented native ELF-linker support for -Wl,<opt> (rpath/soname/--start-group/
    --as-needed/--no-undefined/-v/-z), -nodefaultlibs, -nostdlib, and -r, and for
    linking only objects and libs instead of falling back to the external linker.
  - Implemented native macho linker for executables with external symbols.
  - Native PE linker: COFF objects are read in binary mode (a text-mode read
    rejected any object containing a 0x0d 0x0a byte pair and silently forced
    the external gcc.exe: 464 of the tcc/unit/c-testsuite links under wine,
    about 180 now), ___chkstk_ms is synthesized by the linker (no libgcc
    needed for frames of 4 KiB or more), the stack reserve is 2 MiB like
    GNU ld, and weak-external definitions (plain C99 inline bodies) stay
    weak so &fn is identical across TUs. Links needing libmingwex/
    libwinpthread symbols, -Wl,* (except --out-implib), -nostdlib, -r,
    sanitizers or -static still use the external linker.

  - Windows -O2: implicit-int calls keep the full pointer result, variadic
    register arguments are also stored in the home area, and rdi is not kept
    across calls (AST-compiled callees clobber it).
  - New -save-temps (also -save-temps=cwd): keep the per-file objects of a
    compile+link as <name>.o in the current directory. The RCC_KEEP_TMP
    environment variable, which only printed a debug line, is gone, and the
    native linkers' fallback traces are now enabled with -Wl,-v instead of
    the RCC_LINK_DEBUG environment variable.
  - New -fsanitize=<list>: address, undefined, float-cast-overflow.
    `undefined` instruments divide-by-zero, signed overflow, shift
    out-of-range, null deref, null C-string argument, pointer-overflow,
    alignment, local array bounds, returns-nonnull-attribute, and reached
    __builtin_unreachable; `float-cast-overflow` (a separate flag, not in
    `undefined`) checks float->int cast overflow; `address` checks global
    out-of-bounds on x86-64. All link the gcc sanitizer runtime. About
    10x faster compilation than with gcc or clang, so ideal for debugging
    large programs; like my 140MB libredwg shared library. 2m vs 20m does
    make a difference.

* Changes in v1.2.2 (2026-09-19) (Reini Urban)

  - New -O1 codegen passes: local value propagation with dead-store
    elimination, jump threading, cheap algebraic-identity folds, and
    dense small-int switch → jump table lowering. Sparse switches
    (>=150 exact-value cases, benchmarked breakeven) now lower to a
    balanced binary search or a perfect-hash dispatch instead of a
    linear cmp/jcc chain.
  - New -O2 single-scalar-arg/return tail-call optimization on
    x86-64 and ARM64 (ND_RETURN tail-jmp under -finline+-funroll),
    with frame-escape and __builtin_* safety checks.
  - Added FreeBSD, NetBSD and OpenBSD support, all three built and
    tested in CI (vmactions QEMU VMs) as mandatory, blocking jobs —
    no `continue-on-error`. Also added a `clang`-built matrix job on
    Linux (rcc itself is compiler-agnostic; only the build toolchain
    changes).
  - Fixed a real, universal codegen bug found via FreeBSD CI: an
    `extern __thread` variable owned by a separate shared library
    (e.g. libc's own TLS state, reached through an inlined libc
    header) got the unsafe direct-offset (local-exec) TLS
    relocation, valid only for TLS vars owned by the same link unit.
    Now uses the GOT-indirected initial-exec model whenever the
    variable is extern/weak, on every platform.
  - Fixed a real ELF-writer bug found via OpenBSD/FreeBSD CI (both
    use LLVM's `ld.lld` as the system linker): a SHF_MERGE section's
    on-disk size wasn't padded to a multiple of its declared
    `sh_entsize`, which the ELF spec requires and `ld.lld` correctly
    enforces (`ld.bfd` silently tolerates the violation, masking it
    on Linux). Affected real code, e.g. the Linux kernel's
    `ALTERNATIVE()` macro's `.altinstructions` table.
  - Fixed undefined behavior in the lexer: `isspace`/`isdigit`/
    `isxdigit`/`ispunct` were called on signed `char` values instead
    of `(unsigned char)`, undefined for any UTF-8 byte ≥0x80. glibc
    tolerates it silently; NetBSD's stricter libc didn't, crashing
    on any non-ASCII identifier.
  - Fixed a test-harness crash (not rcc itself) on NetBSD: a
    diagnostic re-run path passed a literal NULL as `argv[0]` to
    `posix_spawnp()`, which NetBSD's libc dereferences and segfaults
    on.
  - rcc's native ELF linker falls back to the system `cc`/`ld` on
    every BSD for cases it doesn't special-case yet (BSD `crt1.o`
    layout, `PT_INTERP`/`DT_NEEDED` naming) — tracked in `TODO`.

* Changes in v1.2.1 (2026-09-16) (Reini Urban)

  - Compile-time performance: faster lexer (ASCII fast path), faster
    linker (indexed/DT_HASH symbol lookups instead of rescanning
    .dynsym per symbol), faster preprocessor (concat_strings() relinks
    tokens instead of cloning the stream); better benchmarks
  - Added test/gcc-bugs/, a suite of ~600 reproducers for GCC's open
    bug tracker.
  - Fixed a parser segfault on sizeof() with unbalanced parens and
    several further gcc-bugs regressions (const-init, vector_size,
    enum attribute, atomic pointer, __has_builtin, alignas).
  - Implemented sqrt() on a vector-of-2-doubles, 3 missing GCC
    diagnostics, duplicate-function-definition detection, and the GCC
    extension accepting a missing ';' at the end of a struct/union.
  - New optimizations: generalized AST constant folding covers
    bitwise/shift/compare/unary/?: on compile-time constants (not
    just `+ - * / %`); do-while(0) macro-body flattening at -O1+;
    and a new src/cg_opt.c does x86-64/ARM64 constant-divisor
    idiv/div strength reduction (shifts and Hacker's Delight/LLVM
    magic-number multiplies) for signed and unsigned 32/64-bit
    division and modulo.
  - Added csmith differential fuzzing diffing -O0/-O1/-O2/-O3 codegen
    against each other and reference compilers. Found and fixed 9 register-
    allocator/spiller aliasing miscompilations (struct-copy and
    struct-assign, ternary and pre/post-increment addressing,
    bitfield read-modify-write, cmp-lhs and %rsi clobbers across
    gen_funcall argument staging, Win64 variadic frame overflow,
    ARM64 global-load aliasing, register-pressure undercounting),
    each with a regression test under test/test_csmith_*.c.
  - Fixed 3 -O2-only regressions found by bisecting the full -O2
    suite (try_inline() signedness mismatch, try_unroll() early-exit
    induction-variable value, float-to-int cast saturation
    mismatch), plus a _Complex comparison register leak in
    gen_cond_branch_inv.
  - Fixed ND_ASSIGN narrow-type truncation and array-not-assignable
    diagnostics, a pack(1) bitfield global-initializer truncation
    past 4 bytes, and an overflowing snprintf() call.
  - Fixed libdir lookup with installed rcc. Fixed arm64-elf GOT
    load for extern function address-of.
  - Faster `make install` (only reinstalls changed files). Test
    harness: run_tests --timeout now also bounds the compiler
    process, per-compiler timeouts and --no-<compiler> skip flags
    for test-all-compilers.sh, rv-match kcc and blitzy/ccc-fork
    support, and a gnulib-style news-check + mk-release release
    tool.

* Changes in v1.2.0 (2026-09-04) (Reini Urban)

  - New linux/elf x86_64 and darwin-aarch64 backends, native
    assembler and linker.
  - Also a much faster preprocessor, which doesn't retokenize, but
    dumps the pp tokens only on -E to a string.
  - New test suites from ncc, c-testsuite and gcc torture, running in
    parallel. Also many more unit-tests for all fixed bugs.
  - New bench suites for middle sized, and larged sized code.
  - Fixed all the bugs, and added all C23 and C29 features.
  - Added a comprehensive C compiler test overview table.
  - Compiles the linux kernel and all ~270 slimcc third_party test
    projects.
  - Compiled code is slower, but more correct than with all other
    compilers. And it compiles almost as fast as tinycc, with just
    much more features.

